\cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The corner-as-signpost idea is strong. It solves the problem that colour alone gives you 
\f3\i \strokec2 where you are
\f2\i0 \strokec2  but not 
\f3\i \strokec2 which way to go
\f2\i0 \strokec2 , and a band on each face of a corner reads like a fingerpost without any text. It's also deniable: to a guest it's a decorative reveal or a protective corner guard.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \strokec2 What makes it work
\f2\b0 \strokec2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Directionality comes from the pair.
\f2\b0  One colour on each side of a corner says "this way is X, that way is Y." Staff would read it unconsciously while turning the corner, which suits a place where nobody is ever meant to ask directions.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The door-jamb strip adds a second tier.
\f2\b0  Corners say which department you're heading toward, and the jamb says you've arrived. Two scales of signal also give Halo something to cross-check: if the corner says amber and the jamb says amber, the system is confirmed.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Lipstick hues are a good key.
\f2\b0  They're sophisticated, closely related colours (nudes, berries, corals, wines), which makes them hard to tell apart without training. A guest sees a tasteful palette, while a staffer knows "dusty rose" from "dried rose." It's also very Eloise: the nail polish is already established as her signature, and lipstick makes her a woman who colour-codes a building like a vanity table.}